.TH WISP.CONF 5 "2026-09-15" "wisp 0.1.0" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
wisp.conf \- runtime configuration, module selection and key providers of a wisp initramfs
.SH DESCRIPTION
The builder,
.BR wisp (8),
reads three files under
.IR /etc/wisp .
None of them is required.
.SS /etc/wisp/wisp.conf
Lines of
.IR key " = " value ;
.B #
starts a comment; unknown keys are ignored and never stop a boot. The builder
completes the file (see below) and places the completed copy at
.I /etc/wisp/wisp.conf
inside the initramfs, where it is part of the archive bytes and therefore of
whatever measures the archive. The kernel command line is consulted only for
values the file does not set:
.BR root= ", " rootfstype= ", " rd.luks.uuid= ", " rd.luks.name= .
.TP
.BI key " = none | passphrase | credential | " name
How the volume key is obtained.
.B none
skips LUKS entirely and mounts the root file system directly.
.B passphrase
asks on the console, three attempts.
.B credential
reads
.I /.extra/credentials/cryptsetup.passphrase
from the initramfs, the file a pre-kernel stage may append as an extra cpio
archive (the systemd-stub credential convention); the file is unlinked after
reading, and when it is missing the passphrase prompt is the recovery path.
Any other
.I name
is a key provider, see below. Default: passphrase when
.I /etc/crypttab
has a LUKS entry, none otherwise.
.TP
.BI luks.uuid " = " uuid
The LUKS2 container. Taken from the first LUKS entry of
.I /etc/crypttab
when absent.
.TP
.BI luks.name " = " name
The device-mapper name of the opened container. Taken from the running
system or from
.I /etc/crypttab
when absent; default
.BR root .
.TP
.BI luks.header " = " path
A detached header copy. The file is placed in the initramfs at the same path
and used instead of the header on the device.
.TP
.BI root " = " device " | UUID=" uuid
The root file system. Taken from the running system when absent.
.TP
.BI rootfstype " = " type
Optional; the type is otherwise probed from the superblock (ext4, xfs, btrfs).
.SS /etc/wisp/modules
One entry per line: a module
.I name
(its dependencies are resolved), or a directory selector against the path
inside
.IR /lib/modules/ version :
.B +dir:
.I subtree
includes every module under it,
.B \-dir:
.I subtree
removes a subtree again and wins over
.B +dir
regardless of line order. Subtrees match whole path components. Cutouts shape
only the selected set; a dependency that lives in an excluded subtree is still
pulled in when a selected module needs it. Without this file the selection is, in
the spirit of initramfs-tools' "most", every module of the kernel except the
subtrees that cannot matter before the root file system is mounted (the firmware
frame buffer driver, found by its platform alias in the target kernel, survives the gpu
exclusion): network,
sound, GPUs, media, staging, InfiniBand, Bluetooth, IIO sensors, hwmon, the
kheaders module and ZFS. About 3200 modules and 53 MB compressed on an Ubuntu
kernel.
.PP
Modules are loaded at boot only when their alias matches present hardware, so
declaring many costs archive size, not boot time.
.SS /etc/wisp/lvm.tab
The device-mapper table of the root logical volume, as
.B dmsetup table
prints it, with the backing device replaced by
.BR @crypt@ ,
and optionally the device-mapper UUID so that lvm2 recognises the device:
.PP
.nf
    name ubuntu--vg-ubuntu--lv
    uuid LVM-iv3wXCsV3yUB7wsXDyWJuufKHhl2eUQkTKFRwz3pnt4DBUAqdWTe5wX2jRlrkBh0
    0 77328384 linear @crypt@ 2048
.fi
.PP
Without this file the builder records the table from the running system when
the root file system is a linear logical volume directly over the dm-crypt
device. Any other topology (several physical volumes, striped, thin, RAID,
LVM without LUKS) is rejected at build time. After resizing the root volume,
rebuild the initramfs.
.SH KEY PROVIDERS
A key provider is an executable
.IR /usr/lib/wisp/key/ name
with an optional configuration directory
.IR /etc/wisp/key/ name / .
When
.B key
names a provider, the builder places both in the initramfs at the same paths.
At boot,
.BR wisp-init (7)
runs the provider once. Protocol version 1:
.TP
arguments
.BI "\-\-protocol 1 \-\-device " "luks-device" " \-\-name " dm-name
.TP
environment
.BR WISP_PROTOCOL=1 ", " WISP_LUKS_DEVICE ", " WISP_LUKS_NAME ", " PATH
.TP
file descriptors
0, 1 and 2 are the console: the provider runs its own dialogue. 3 is the write
end of a pipe: the provider writes the raw key bytes (1 to 512) and exits 0.
.TP
exit status
0 success; 10 wrong secret; 11 state or policy mismatch; 12 hardware missing;
13 aborted by the user; anything else is a failure. The init halts on every
non-zero status and does not retry.
.PP
The provider process owns everything sensitive it computes and has ended
before the container is opened. Provider names are lower-case, up to 32
characters of letters, digits, hyphen and underscore.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR wisp (8),
.BR wisp-init (7),
.BR crypttab (5)
